-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/ea06e94f-cbe1-50e2-9d00-58cdf44c0c46
-- title:
--   Power series presentation from special-fibre coordinates
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring and $p$ a prime such that $p$ is a non-zero-divisor in $\mathcal O$, such that $\mathcal O$ carries an algebra structure over $\mathbb Z/p$ whose structure map has kernel exactly the ideal $(p)$, and such that $\mathcal O$ is adically complete (and separated) for $(p)$. Let $R_0(v)$, $v\in\mathbb N$, be commutative $\mathcal O$-algebras, each finite and free as an $\mathcal O$-module, together with $\mathcal O$-algebra maps $t_v\colon R_0(v+1)\to R_0(v)$. Let $\sigma$ be a finite type and let $x_{v,i}\in R_0(v)$ ($i\in\sigma$) satisfy: $t_v(x_{v+1,i})=x_{v,i}$; each $x_{v,i}$ lies in the radical of the ideal generated by $p$ in $R_0(v)$; for each $v$ the $\mathbb Z/p$-algebra map $\mathbb Z/p[X_i : i\in\sigma]\to (\mathbb Z/p)\otimes_{\mathcal O}R_0(v)$ sending $X_i\mapsto 1\otimes x_{v,i}$ is surjective; and for every $N$ there is a $v$ for which the kernel of that map is contained in the $N$-th power of the ideal generated by the variables $X_i$. Then there exist $\mathcal O$-algebra maps $\pi_v\colon \mathcal O[[X_i : i\in\sigma]]\to R_0(v)$ such that $\pi_v(X_i)=x_{v,i}$; each $\pi_v$ is surjective; $\pi_{v+1}$ followed by $t_v$ equals $\pi_v$; each $\pi_v(X_i)$ lies in the radical of $(p)\subseteq R_0(v)$; each $\pi_v$ is given by [`MvFormalGroup.adicEval`](def/MvFormalGroup_PointsV2.html#L20) for the ideal generated by $p$ in $R_0(v)$ at the points $\pi_v(X_i)$, that is, by $(p)$-adically convergent evaluation $\mathrm{eval}_2$ of the structure map $\mathcal O\to R_0(v)$ at those points, $\mathcal O$ being taken discrete; a power series annihilated by all $\pi_v$ is $0$; and every family $(z_v)$ with $t_v(z_{v+1})=z_v$ is of the form $(\pi_v(G))_v$ for some $G$.
--
--   This is the second half of Tate's argument that a connected $p$-divisible group over a suitable base is a formal Lie group, in the form "$R/pR$ a power series ring over $\mathbb F_p$ plus $p$-adic completeness and $p$-torsion-freeness imply $R$ is a power series ring over $\mathcal O$", stated levelwise for a tower so that no inverse-limit ring appears and with no Hopf or divisibility structure assumed; the last two clauses say precisely that $G\mapsto(\pi_v G)_v$ identifies $\mathcal O[[X_i : i\in\sigma]]$ with $\varprojlim_v R_0(v)$. It is used to equip the tower coming from a connected $p$-divisible group with formal coordinates, in [`PDivisibleGroup.exists_mvFormalGroup_comul_eq_adicEval_of_specialFibre_coordinates`](thm.html#PDivisibleGroup.exists_mvFormalGroup_comul_eq_adicEval_of_specialFibre_coordinates).
-- source:
